      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =5\\ne 0, \\\\ {y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =-5\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/id": "53ac4373-de1a-5280-bf26-a19a0f21c857"
    },
