      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=14(t+1)^{6},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2.\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n14(t+1)^{6}=0\\,⟹\\,t=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that ${y}^{′}(-1)=2\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t=-1,$ the tangent to the curve is vertical.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/id": "20fd264f-a124-52c8-a486-ea08b973659d"
    },
