  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186083278,
      "id": "3fc81cba-b997-54bc-9e11-0a4ff130833e",
      "title": "Determining the Points Where the Tangent is Horizontal or Vertical"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3,\\,y=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-2\\pi \\le t<0.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/value": "$t=-\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ only",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/text",
              ":answer/id": "227507d0-5770-5dc7-8e51-a2e7291278b1"
            },
            {
              ":answer/value": "$t=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ only",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/text",
              ":answer/id": "4fda1870-3119-52ff-883e-6db9804ad6d1"
            },
            {
              ":answer/value": "$t=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ only",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/text",
              ":answer/id": "83cc3564-9a65-54ab-9b59-d4dc4fb5efde"
            },
            {
              ":answer/value": "$t=-\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ and $t=-\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/text",
              ":answer/id": "0719cd3e-6674-585a-b628-997e522bce40"
            },
            {
              ":answer/value": "$t=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ and $t=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/text",
              ":answer/id": "d6ac8cdc-9794-57f3-8fba-fe1dc45e764b"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/id": "12b6c122-8ffe-5ac7-8afa-daf4996eee9c",
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "d6ac8cdc-9794-57f3-8fba-fe1dc45e764b"
          ]
        }
      ],
      ":question/worked-solution": "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=0\\,⟹\\,t=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =3\\ne 0, \\\\ {y}^{′}(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ and $t=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/id": "4e1a092d-0dd6-504e-8bd8-6ba28b96783b"
    },
